  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with ridging over the Great Plains and an \narea of high pressure over the Gulf Coast. Cloud cover continues to \nlinger across northeastern Nebraska early and northwestern Iowa this \nafternoon, keeping temperatures in the 70s to low 80s. Expected high \ntemperatures today will be in the 70s to mid-80s for areas along and \nnorth of a line from David City, NE to Harlan, IA. Areas south of \nthis line can expect highs ranging from the mid-80s to mid-90s. \nReturn flow is bringing some moisture into the region which will \nplay a factor in our next chance for storms. \n\nOur next chance of storms (20-30%) will be late this afternoon into \nearly evening ahead of our next storm system. Surface low pressure \nover western Kansas will lift to the northeast through the afternoon \nand evening along with a warm front currently situated near the \nKS/NE border. SBCAPE values of 2500+ J/kg, MLCAPE of 3000+ J/kg, and \nDCAPE of 1000+ J/kg are expected. Pwat values of 1/7+ are also \nexpected. Should any storms be able to develop this \nafternoon/evening, some hail and wind would be expected. As the low \nmoves into the region overnight, we will have better chances for \nshower/thunderstorm activity across the region, with the best \nchances after midnight. Damaging wind gusts, heavy rain, and \nlocalized flooding will again be a concern with overnight/Friday \nmorning convection.\n\nThunderstorm chances will continue through the morning and into the \nearly afternoon on Friday across portions of the area, tapering off \nwest to east. High temperatures will once again be variable based on \nlocation, with cooler temperatures in the northern CWA where cloud \ncover and potential storm chances could impact our ability to warm \nup. \n\nSlightly warmer temperatures are expected Saturday (low to mid-80s \nnorth; mid-80s to low 90s south). Southwest flow sets up with high \npressure over the Gulf Coast states. Return flow brings moisture \ninto the region and once again, there will be chances for showers \nand storms through the day as a cold front drops in in from the \nDakotas. \n\nWe'll enjoy a short-lived cool down Sunday with highs ranging from \nthe mid-70s in the north to the mid-80s along the KS/MO/NE/IA \nborder. Sunday will once again bring a chance for some showers and \nstorms.",
